# This checkout has core.autocrlf=true (Windows), which normalises text
# files to CRLF on checkout. That is fine for source read by an editor or by
# node/python given an explicit interpreter, but any file the Linux appliance
# runs BY ITS SHEBANG breaks the moment a stray carriage-return character
# lands at the end of its first line: bash reads the interpreter line as
# corrupted and every following line keeps its trailing carriage return,
# which bash treats as part of the command; a shebang'd python3/node script
# gets a "no such file or directory" from /usr/bin/env instead of running.
#
# Concretely: a fresh Windows checkout turned setup/*.sh into CRLF and broke
# harness/trustAnchor (2026-09-23). setup/release.mjs already re-normalises
# every shipped .sh (and .py, .mjs, ...) to LF when it stages a release
# tarball (see TEXT_EXTENSIONS in setup/release.mjs), so the appliance itself
# is never exposed to this — but the WORKING TREE copy that bash/python run
# directly, in a harness or by hand on the box, is not staged, so it must be
# forced to LF at checkout instead of trusted to arrive that way.
#
# Scope: every shell script (wherever it lives), plus the two detector/
# scripts that carry a shebang and the executable bit for direct invocation
# on the Linux box. The other detector/*.py files are plain modules run via
# `python3 -m pytest` / an explicit `python3 script.py` (see
# agent/detect-service.mjs), so CRLF in their body does not stop them running
# and they are left to core.autocrlf like the rest of the source.
*.sh text eol=lf
detector/replay.py text eol=lf
detector/yolox_worker.py text eol=lf
